/// A Weave service as a WebAssembly component: Weave runs it inside itself, in a sandbox, under the service's own
/// key and with exactly its contract's rights. It needs nothing installed (no Docker), and gets only what Weave hands
/// it: these functions, a folder of its own (/data), clocks and randomness, and HTTPS (wasi:http) to the hosts its
/// contract names in "net", none on the node's own networks. No other network, no other files, no processes.
///
/// Values that carry Weave's own data (items, bodies, who it is) are JSON, so any language can read and write them.
package weave:service@0.1.0;

/// What Weave offers the service: who it is, and acting as itself within its contract.
interface host {
  /// Who the service is: {name, did, person, contract, spaces: [{id, kind, name, settings}]}.
  me: func() -> string;
  /// A setting its person gave it (sealed on the node that runs it), if there is one.
  setting: func(name: string) -> option<string>;
  /// Sign and publish an event in one of its spaces (by id or name), of a kind its contract writes, with a JSON body:
  /// the new item (JSON), or why not.
  publish: func(space: string, kind: string, body: string) -> result<string, string>;
  /// Answer a request addressed to it (text, data as JSON, or an error): the answer (JSON), or why not.
  answer: func(request: string, text: option<string>, data: option<string>, error: option<string>) -> result<string, string>;
  /// What it saved under this name in its /data folder (a-z, 0-9, ., _ and -), if anything: for languages without
  /// file access (JavaScript); others can use /data directly.
  load: func(name: string) -> option<string>;
  /// Keep something under this name in its /data folder (what it keeps between runs).
  save: func(name: string, value: string) -> result<_, string>;
  /// A line for its log (weave service log).
  log: func(message: string);
  /// Every this many milliseconds (at least 1000; 0 stops it), on-event gets {"kind": "weave.tick", "time"}: for work
  /// nobody asked for (keeping something in step). A tick waits while the last one is still being handled.
  tick-every: func(ms: u32);
  /// Sign data with a key among its settings, the key never reaching it: "RS256" (an RSA key, PEM), "ES256" (a P-256
  /// key, PEM) or "HS256" (any secret). A setting its manifest marks "signing" is only usable this way: setting()
  /// doesn't return it. The signature (ES256 as r||s, as JWTs have it), or why not.
  sign: func(setting: string, algorithm: string, data: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, string>;
  /// A release of one of its spaces as Weave knows it (published there by a maintainer), by its id: {id, tag, name,
  /// manifest} as JSON, or nothing. Only for a service that offers artifact.fetch: it hands those releases' files out,
  /// and only theirs, whatever a request names.
  release: func(space: string, id: string) -> option<string>;
}

world service {
  import host;
  /// Something it may read arrived, as a feed item (JSON): a request addressed to it (kind weave.service.request,
  /// body {to, text?, data?}), or what its contract reads. One at a time, in order; also what came while it was off.
  export on-event: func(item: string);
}

/// The players of a website it serves: Weave holds their connections, and tells it who each one is.
interface players {
  /// Send a message to one player's connection (by its id, from on-join).
  send: func(player: string, message: string);
  /// Send a message to every player connected now.
  broadcast: func(message: string);
  /// End a player's connection.
  disconnect: func(player: string);
  /// Be called (on-tick) every this many milliseconds, at least 50; 0 stops it.
  tick-every: func(ms: u32);
}

/// A service with a website: a game, a board, a page with live parts. Weave serves its files (its site folder) and
/// holds its visitors' connections (WebSockets at /weave/socket): it only handles what they say. The service's
/// contract says website: true.
world website {
  import host;
  import players;
  export on-event: func(item: string);
  /// A player connected: {id, did, name?, host, member, maintainer, room?: {id, name}}, as Weave vouches for them.
  export on-join: func(player: string);
  /// A message from a player (by id).
  export on-message: func(player: string, message: string);
  /// A player's connection ended.
  export on-leave: func(player: string);
  /// Its timer (tick-every).
  export on-tick: func();
}

/// A client service: a board's lens, run by every member's own node. Pure: it gets nothing from Weave but what it's
/// called with (no clock, no randomness, no network), so every member folds the same state from the same events.
/// It has no key: what its actions propose, the member signs, if their rights in the board allow it. Its site (the
/// renderer) shows the state in a sandboxed frame and asks for actions; agents get the same state and actions.
world board {
  /// The board's state, from the board ({name, title}, JSON) and its items (a JSON array of feed items, oldest first:
  /// its posts, their edits, deletions and votes, and the messages in their chats). The state is the service's own
  /// (JSON); Weave reads only its "actions": [{name, label, input?}], what can be done with it.
  export fold: func(board: string, items: string) -> string;
  /// What an action proposes: events for the member to sign, as JSON [{kind, body}], or why not. The input is what the
  /// renderer or agent gave (JSON), the state as fold made it, and me who asks ({did, name}).
  export act: func(name: string, input: string, state: string, me: string) -> result<string, string>;
}
